@startuml linkmap
'
' Engineered by  Christian Schnapka
'                Embedded Principal+ Engineer
'                Macstab GmbH · Hamburg, Germany
'                https://macstab.com
'
' ELF lazy binding via PLT/GOT under LD_PRELOAD interposition: when the
' real-function table is resolved, the first call (cold path) and every
' later call (hot path).
'
' Reference: docs/PLATFORM.md §1.2-§1.4
'            src/core/chaos_io.c (chaos_io_ensure_symbols), src/common/chaos_once.h
'
title ELF lazy binding via PLT/GOT — first call (cold) vs hot path

skinparam sequenceMessageAlign center
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam roundCorner 4
skinparam sequence {
  ArrowColor                 #2C3E50
  LifeLineBorderColor        #2C3E50
  LifeLineBackgroundColor    #ECF0F1
  ParticipantBorderColor     #2C3E50
  ParticipantBackgroundColor #FFFFFF
  ParticipantFontStyle       bold
}
hide footbox

participant "Application\ncode"            as App
participant "read@plt"                     as PLT
participant "GOT[read]"                    as GOT
participant "ld.so /\n_dl_runtime_resolve" as Ldso
participant "do_lookup_x()"                as Lookup
participant "libchaos-io.so:read"          as Chaos
participant "libc:read"                    as Glibc

== Load time ==
Ldso   ->  Chaos  : constructor: ensure_symbols()
note right of Chaos
  chaos_once: dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, …) for the whole
  real-function table, exactly once per process.
  A wrapper that runs before the constructor
  (another object's constructor) triggers it
  instead; concurrent callers sched_yield()
  until it is done.
end note

== First call — cold path (lazy binding) ==
App    ->  PLT    : call read
PLT    ->  GOT    : jmp *GOT[read]\n(resolver stub)
GOT    ->  Ldso   : bounce into resolver
Ldso   ->  Lookup : walk the global scope
note over Lookup
  Order:
  1. main executable
  2. LD_PRELOAD entries, left to right
  3. DT_NEEDED, breadth-first
  First definition wins.
end note
Lookup --> Ldso   : found in libchaos-io.so
Ldso   ->  GOT    : patch slot → libchaos-io.so:read
Ldso   ->  Chaos  : jump to wrapper
Chaos  ->  Chaos  : ensure_symbols() (done: one acquire load)\nmatch rules, maybe inject
Chaos  ->  Glibc  : real_read(fd, buf, n)\n(pointer cached at load, guard set)
Glibc  --> Chaos  : result
Chaos  --> App    : return

== Second call onward — hot path ==
App    ->  PLT    : call read
PLT    ->  GOT    : jmp *GOT[read]\n(now → wrapper)
GOT    ->  Chaos  : direct
Chaos  ->  Chaos  : ensure_symbols(), match rules, maybe inject
Chaos  ->  Glibc  : real_read(fd, buf, n)
Glibc  --> Chaos  : result
Chaos  --> App    : return

note across
  With -z now or LD_BIND_NOW the loader fills GOT[read] at load time and the cold path
  is skipped; interposition is identical. Hot-path cost of interposition itself: one
  indirect branch through the GOT slot and one indirect call through the cached
  real-function pointer. No dlsym runs per call.
end note

@enduml
